WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.460 --> 00:00:07.919
There's little reason to fear
videogames will cause a catastrophic

00:00:07.919 --> 00:00:12.120
labor shortage. First of all, it's a red
flag that the big gaps in hours

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:15.389
and employment between younger and older
men emerged during the Great Recession

00:00:15.389 --> 00:00:18.900
and Not-So-Great Recovery. They're
plenty of potential non-gaming

00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:23.039
explanations. For instance, during a time
of economic unrest, employers may have

00:00:23.039 --> 00:00:27.990
demanded more education and experience
from potential employees. Moreover, if the

00:00:27.990 --> 00:00:31.740
recession recovery played a big role in
young men working less, then work rates

00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:35.489
should improve the further we move into the economic expansion. And guess what:

00:00:35.489 --> 00:00:39.450
That's exactly what seems to be happening.
It's also unclear what role video games

00:00:39.450 --> 00:00:43.710
play in long-term employment trends for
young men. Since 2000, their

00:00:43.710 --> 00:00:47.219
employment-to-population ratio has
fallen by about 10 percentage points in

00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:51.060
the United States. But in Japan, where
presumably they play a lot of video games,

00:00:51.060 --> 00:00:55.199
the work ratio has actually increased
a bit over that same span. The big

00:00:55.199 --> 00:00:59.219
problem with the U.S. economy over the
past decade isn't gaming any more than

00:00:59.219 --> 00:01:02.250
it's automation or trade. It's the fact
that America suffered a massive

00:01:02.250 --> 00:01:06.320
financial shock and near depression, and
it just takes a while for economies to recover.

00:01:07.380 --> 00:01:10.500
Do you think video games keep
young men from working? let us know in

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:14.220
your comments. Also, let us know what other topics you'd like our scholars to cover

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:20.240
in 60 seconds, and be sure to subscribe
for more research and videos from AEI.

